II.** At every point along the line $y=0,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- **III.** At every point along the line $y=-2,$ the slope field has a negative slope" "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186253568 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,2)}=-2<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,2)$ is negative for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,2)$ is equal to $-2$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,0)}=-0=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,-2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-2)}=-(-2)=2>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is equal to $2$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8f/8fc0b5be5c9147c29410ff7a082316403302f2bce7ec25c665ab7ccc5bf90b0f.png)" "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement “At every point along the line $y=2,$ the slope field has a positive slope” is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,2)}=-2<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,2)$ is negative for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,2)$ is equal to $-2$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement “At every point along the line $y=0,$ the slope field is horizontal” is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,0)}=-0=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\n- Statement “At every point along the line $y=-2,$ the slope field has a negative slope” is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,-2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-2)}=-(-2)=2>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is equal to $2$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “At every point along the line $y=0,$ the slope field is horizontal” only.\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8f/8fc0b5be5c9147c29410ff7a082316403302f2bce7ec25c665ab7ccc5bf90b0f.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186449085 :answer-field/key "I" "At every point along the line $y=2,$ the slope field has a positive slope"] [:db/cas 17592186449086 :answer-field/key "II" "At every point along the line $y=0,$ the slope field is horizontal"] [:db/cas 17592186449087 :answer-field/key "III" "At every point along the line $y=-2,$ the slope field has a negative slope"] [:db/cas 17592186253586 :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,-3),(0,5),(2,-5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n- **I.** $(3,-2)$\n- **II.** $(-5,0)$\n- **III.** $(5,2)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,-3),(0,5),(2,-5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186253586 :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(3,-2),(-5,0),(5,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(3,-2),(-5,0),(5,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is “The point $(3,-2)$”; “The point $(-5,0)$”; “The point $(5,2)$”."] [:db/cas 17592186462688 :answer-field/key "I" "The point $(3,-2)$"] [:db/cas 17592186462689 :answer-field/key "II" "The point $(-5,0)$"] [:db/cas 17592186462690 :answer-field/key "III" "The point $(5,2)$"] [:db/cas 17592186253588 :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-1),(-1,2),(2,-3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under a **clockwise** rotation of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians about the origin?\n- **I.** $(0,\\sqrt{2})$\n- **II.** $(3,-1)$\n- **III.** $(-5,1)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-1),(-1,2),(2,-3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under a **clockwise** rotation of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians about the origin?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186253588 :question/worked-solution "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into the above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the transformation, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the rotation $R$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{ }\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are \n\n$$\n(0,\\sqrt{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into the above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the transformation, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the rotation $R$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{ }\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are \n\n$$\n(0,\\sqrt{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is “The point $(0,\\sqrt{2})$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186462691 :answer-field/key "I" "The point $(0,\\sqrt{2})$"] [:db/cas 17592186462692 :answer-field/key "II" "The point $(3,-1)$"] [:db/cas 17592186462693 :answer-field/key "III" "The point $(-5,1)$"] [:db/cas 17592186253592 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe quadrilateral $Q$ is mapped to the quadrilateral ${Q}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The quadrilateral ${Q}^{′}$ has vertices $(-2,-2),(-3,2),(2,2),$ and $(-1,-1).$ Which of the following are vertices of $Q?$\n- **I.** $(2,2)$\n- **II.** $(0,1)$\n- **III.** $(-5,-8)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe quadrilateral $Q$ is mapped to the quadrilateral ${Q}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The quadrilateral ${Q}^{′}$ has vertices $(-2,-2),(-3,2),(2,2),$ and $(-1,-1).$ Which of the following are vertices of $Q?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186253592 :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${Q}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $Q$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-1-(-2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -8 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $Q$ are $(0,-2),(-5,-8),(0,2),(0,-1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${Q}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $Q$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-1-(-2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -8 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $Q$ are $(0,-2),(-5,-8),(0,2),(0,-1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The point $(-5,-8)$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186462694 :answer-field/key "I" "The point $(2,2)$"] [:db/cas 17592186462695 :answer-field/key "II" "The point $(0,1)$"] [:db/cas 17592186462696 :answer-field/key "III" "The point $(-5,-8)$"] [:db/cas 17592186253595 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=6xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n- **II.** At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **III.** At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=6xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186253595 :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=6xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/6e/6ef852c9c15dc6f591119906abe479949f929edb6dc26f21588b4de545d02da2.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the third quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "First, we define $f(x,y)=6xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/6e/6ef852c9c15dc6f591119906abe479949f929edb6dc26f21588b4de545d02da2.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement “At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the third quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative”; “At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive”; “At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative”."] [:db/cas 17592186438808 :answer-field/key "I" "At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative"] [:db/cas 17592186438809 :answer-field/key "II" "At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive"] [:db/cas 17592186438810 :answer-field/key "III" "At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative"] [:db/cas 17592186253597 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-5xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **II.** At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n- **III.** At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-5xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/c