r diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" "First, we define $f(x,y)=(1-x)(y-2).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=1$ or $y=2.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/47/47784a3201e93648e495827b12078073cc9f1a03cd3c11904a875bad10a3bde2.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/73/73631c178777b4489e35fa87d08474ccf2b8b484b9bf5fe7272b1ce25d45f1b0.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<1$ and $y>2,$ the slope of the slope field is negative” is false. If $x<1$ and $y>2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y<2,$ the slope of the slope field is negative” is false. If $x>1$ and $y<2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<1$ and $y<2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive” is false. If $x<1$ and $y<2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""] [:db/cas 17592186438820 :answer-field/key "I" "At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<1$ and $y>2,$ the slope of the slope field is negative"] [:db/cas 17592186438821 :answer-field/key "II" "At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y<2,$ the slope of the slope field is negative"] [:db/cas 17592186438822 :answer-field/key "III" "At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<1$ and $y<2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive"] [:db/cas 17592186253607 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x-2)^{2}(y+1)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<2$ and $y>-1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative\n- **II.** At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<2$ and $y<-1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative\n- **III.** At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>2$ and $y>-1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive" "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x-2)^{2}(y+1)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186253607 :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=(x-2)^{2}(y+1).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=2$ or $y=-1.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/caa61190f1a5565c58838556529715c08f9f00402c6f4b3ccb99a914e748f198.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/d8/d836463631120853e05b3b5fb9e534822cf5611747a3eb17e22333901bfb8fd3.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $x<2$ and $y>-1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $x<2$ and $y<-1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $x>2$ and $y>-1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "First, we define $f(x,y)=(x-2)^{2}(y+1).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=2$ or $y=-1.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/caa61190f1a5565c58838556529715c08f9f00402c6f4b3ccb99a914e748f198.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/d8/d836463631120853e05b3b5fb9e534822cf5611747a3eb17e22333901bfb8fd3.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<2$ and $y>-1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative” is false. If $x<2$ and $y>-1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<2$ and $y<-1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative” is true. If $x<2$ and $y<-1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>2$ and $y>-1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive” is true. If $x>2$ and $y>-1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<2$ and $y<-1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative”; “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>2$ and $y>-1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive” only."] [:db/cas 17592186438823 :answer-field/key "I" "At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<2$ and $y>-1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative"] [:db/cas 17592186438824 :answer-field/key "II" "At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<2$ and $y<-1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative"] [:db/cas 17592186438825 :answer-field/key "III" "At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>2$ and $y>-1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive"] [:db/cas 17592186253631 :question/problem "![](images/17/17cd72acbe8dd412d37c51ff992278d9664f6c5b1141a8604278fda76e57700f.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x-2$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x-2)$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x+1)(x-2)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/17/17cd72acbe8dd412d37c51ff992278d9664f6c5b1141a8604278fda76e57700f.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186253631 :question/worked-solution "![](images/5d/5d6165488c8e41c16cf31b1b000a924344399c2b94a8fe5e7138c55adf97602b.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x-2$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not evaluate to zero at both $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x-2)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x+1)(x-2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=2,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $x=-1,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "![](images/5d/5d6165488c8e41c16cf31b1b000a924344399c2b94a8fe5e7138c55adf97602b.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x-2$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not evaluate to zero at both $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x-2)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x+1)(x-2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=2,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $x=-1,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x-2)$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186462697 :answer-field/key "I" "${y}^{′}=x-2$"] [:db/cas 17592186462698 :answer-field/key "II" "${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x-2)$"] [:db/cas 17592186462699 :answer-field/key "III" "${y}^{′}=x(x+1)(x-2)$"] [:db/cas 17592186253635 :question/problem "![](images/f8/f855062048a7d38b2fa6c843701a182661b2235b2042ad83fbedd600b1dd770c.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{10}(x+3)(x-2)^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{5}(x+3)(2-x)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(x+2)(x-3)$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "![](images/f8/f855062048a7d38b2fa6c843701a182661b2235b2042ad83fbedd600b1dd770c.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186253635 :question/worked-solution "![](images/8f/8fdec9bda9e2bcc514e56a14a6608b2f0cb74fd0943a51c944b29cc047e02cd9.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-3$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-3$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{10}(x+3)(x-2)^{2}$ gives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{5}(x+3)(2-x)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ and $x=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(x+2)(x-3)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "![](images/8f/8fdec9bda9e2bcc514e56a14a6608b2f0cb74fd0943a51c944b29cc047e02cd9.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-3$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-3$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{10}(x+3)(x-2)^{2}$ gives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{5}(x+3)(2-x)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ and $x=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(x+2)(x-3)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{10}(x+3)(x-2)^{2}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186462700 :answer-field/key "I" "${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{10}(x+3)(x-2)^{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186462701 :answer-field/key "II" "${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{5}(x+3)(2-x)$"] [:db/cas 17592186462702 :answer-field/key "III" "${y}^{′}=(x+2)(x-3)$"] [:db/cas 17592186253637 :question/problem "![](images/51/51273365c4a282dc3dedd9d7cd5ac7d805e1526aaeee1915e8bd95d8223c26d3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}+3x$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}+x$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/51/51273365c4a282dc3dedd9d7cd5ac7d805e1526aaeee1915e8bd95d8223c26d3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186253637 :question/worked-solution "![](images/a1/a14651ffc883a8c24ad17bdd031658c3d4ae21db2cfded213f2ba911fdb9a765.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-3$ and $x=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+3x=x(x+3)\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+x=x(x+1)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1$ and $x=0.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(x+3)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ and $x=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "![](images/a1/a14651ffc883a8c24ad17bdd031658c3d4ae21db2cfded213f2ba911fdb9a765.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-3$ and $x=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+3x=x(x+3)\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+x=x(x+1)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1$ and $x=0.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(x+3)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ and $x=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}+3x$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186462703 :answer-field/key "I" "${y}^{′}={x}^{2}+3x$"] [:db/cas 17592186462704 :answer-field/key "II" "${y}^{′}={x}^{2}+x$"] [:db/cas 17592186462705 :answer-field/key "III" "${y}^{′}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186253640 :question/problem "![](images/5f/5fdb2ddd3d07537c1e3d0d22d435928f7cd11dc7a71c67f1bf45292c82a61ef7.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x+1)$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x+1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/5f/5fdb2ddd3d07537c1e3d0d22d435928f7cd11dc7a71c67f1bf45292c82a61ef7.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186253640 :question/worked-solution "![](images/cf/cf2ba44d8cc21ade2204a3784af7d09b492843339a8b98c4cde741b30dfa54db.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=-1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x+1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not approach $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to -1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x+1}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}=\\frac{x}{(1+\\frac{1}{x})}\\to \\pm \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become vertical as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "![](images/cf/cf2ba44d8cc21ade2204a3784af7d09b492843339a8b98c4cde741b30dfa54db.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=-1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x+1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not approach $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to -1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x+1}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}=\\frac{x}{(1+\\frac{1}{x})}\\to \\pm \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become vertical as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186462706 :answer-field/key "I" "${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x+1)$"] [:db/cas 17592186462707 :answer-field/key "II" "${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x+1}$"] [:db/cas 17592186462708 :answer-field/key "III" "${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$"] [:db/cas 17592186253646 :question/problem "![](images/3d/3d24906da8cd181d40dbaec0095b8d51481babc152609fc378b03c61187220c3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)(y-2)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(2-y)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/3d/3d24906da8cd181d40dbaec0095b8d51481babc152609fc378b03c61187220c3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186253646 :question/worked-solution "![](images/7f/7f8a042b9944b2cdf0e864b7dca760f094f4413f197befa05d6e5cbefb3841ba.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-1$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)(y-2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=2,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $y=1,$ which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=\\pm 1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(2-y)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "![](images/7f/7f8a042b9944b2cdf0e864b7dca760f094f4413f197befa05d6e5cbefb3841ba.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-1$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)(y-2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=2,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $y=1,$ which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=\\pm 1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(2-y)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(2-y)$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186462709 :answer-field/key "I" "${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)(y-2)$"] [:db/cas 17592186462710 :answer-field/key "II" "${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)$"] [:db/cas 17592186462711 :answer-field/key "III" "${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(2-y)$"] [:db/cas 17592186253648 :question/problem "Let \n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2} \\\\ 7y\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- **II.** $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- **III.** $T$ is **NOT** a linear transformation\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Let \n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2} \\\\ 7y\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186253648 :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix}=kT\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\  & T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}(kx)^{2} \\\\ 7(ky)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{k}^{2}({x}^{2}) \\\\ k(7y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\begin{bmatrix}k({x}^{2}) \\\\ 7y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne kT\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is not a linear transformation.\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})^{2} \\\\ 7({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ 7{y}_{1}+7{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{2} \\\\ 7{y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 7{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, only statement III is true." "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix}=kT\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\  & T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}(kx)^{2} \\\\ 7(ky)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{k}^{2}({x}^{2}) \\\\ k(7y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\begin{bmatrix}k({x}^{2}) \\\\ 7y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne kT\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is not a linear transformation.\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})^{2} \\\\ 7({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ 7{y}_{1}+7{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{2} \\\\ 7{y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 7{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, only statement “$T$ is **NOT** a linear transformation” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186462712 :answer-field/key "I" "$T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$"] [:db/cas 17592186462713 :answer-field/key "II" "$T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186462714 :answer-field/key "III" "$T$ is **NOT** a linear transformation"] [:db/cas 17592186253649 :question/problem "![](images/d2/d27af23ca398eba62c2ab1fe2ee2cf9bfdf736c16fdd8408d2a13e0d34dae32c.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y)$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y-2)^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y+2)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/d2/d27af23ca398eba62c2ab1fe2ee2cf9bfdf736c16fdd8408d2a13e0d34dae32c.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186253649 :question/worked-solution "![](images/ab/ab076b61b443e031455f07c1e976467cc3b74651bfb5fc2fb39f89a74e156681.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y-2)^{2}$ gives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y+2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-2$ and $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "![](images/ab/ab076b61b443e031455f07c1e976467cc3b74651bfb5fc2fb39f89a74e156681.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y-2)^{2}$ gives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y+2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-2$ and $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=y(y-2)^{2}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186462715 :answer-field/key "I" "${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y)$"] [:db/cas 17592186462716 :answer-field/key "II" "${y}^{′}=y(y-2)^{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186462717 :answer-field/key "III" "${y}^{′}=y(y+2)$"] [:db/cas 17592186253664 :question/problem "![](images/20/204be6af825ef678e06526bff1fdac14ca5f09a2a7bee939620f3f2be1496985.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x(3-y)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(x+3)y$\n- ${y}^{′}=(3-x)y$\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/20/204be6af825ef678e06526bff1fdac14ca5f09a2a7bee939620f3f2be1496985.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186253664 :question/worked-solution "![](images/90/9051213882bcb506ddae46e0a6a79c8cd03a10eadd89e0007f895f68b00c33de.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=3$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x(3-y)$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=3.$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=(x+3)y$ evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=(3-x)y$ evaluates to zero at $x