ated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=4-y$\n- ${y}^{′}=8y-2xy$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}y-8xy+16y$\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/f4/f4a6aa53604ce4381a6627097ba4ebc5853a67ff9fa1675b98e5114775cefdc6.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186256373 :question/worked-solution "![](images/7d/7d0d7ff6fa9c7cd074fcc2321f125ac5da8142e55578459f44903555e2797204.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=4$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=4$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<4$ | $x>4$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4-y\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $y=4$ only.\n\n- Equation II gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8y-2xy=2y(4-x)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=4$ and $y=0,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<4$ | $x>4$ |\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y-8xy+16y=(x-4)^{2}y\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=4$ and $y=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<4$ | $x>4$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "![](images/7d/7d0d7ff6fa9c7cd074fcc2321f125ac5da8142e55578459f44903555e2797204.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=4$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=4$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<4$ | $x>4$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=4-y$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4-y\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $y=4$ only.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=8y-2xy$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8y-2xy=2y(4-x)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=4$ and $y=0,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<4$ | $x>4$ |\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}y-8xy+16y$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y-8xy+16y=(x-4)^{2}y\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=4$ and $y=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<4$ | $x>4$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=8y-2xy$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186462727 :answer-field/key "I" "${y}^{′}=4-y$"] [:db/cas 17592186462728 :answer-field/key "II" "${y}^{′}=8y-2xy$"] [:db/cas 17592186462729 :answer-field/key "III" "${y}^{′}={x}^{2}y-8xy+16y$"] [:db/cas 17592186256374 :question/problem "![](images/26/26a0a41cc50b6d5cb586c52e6a4e6bd5bd324f3e13ad7eef4ee0d9eed18340ea.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=2y-xy$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/26/26a0a41cc50b6d5cb586c52e6a4e6bd5bd324f3e13ad7eef4ee0d9eed18340ea.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186256374 :question/worked-solution "![](images/24/249aba327a8103645963ce95e7935019a279b4cf0d9306111c41172ae22f792b.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=2$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=2$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y-xy=(2-x)y\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=2$ and $y=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=(x+2)^{2}{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=(x-2){y}^{2}\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=2$ and $y=0,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "![](images/24/249aba327a8103645963ce95e7935019a279b4cf0d9306111c41172ae22f792b.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=2$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=2$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=2y-xy$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y-xy=(2-x)y\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=2$ and $y=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=(x+2)^{2}{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=(x-2){y}^{2}\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=2$ and $y=0,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186462730 :answer-field/key "I" "${y}^{′}=2y-xy$"] [:db/cas 17592186462731 :answer-field/key "II" "${y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186462732 :answer-field/key "III" "${y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186256376 :question/problem "![](images/85/85fcea0ad26f7da26189c7611a851bfe228d3ad5d428dcba6c8299c8304540d1.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=(x-1){y}^{2}-x+1$\n- ${y}^{′}=xy-y-(x-1)$\n- ${y}^{′}=y-1-x(y-1)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/85/85fcea0ad26f7da26189c7611a851bfe228d3ad5d428dcba6c8299c8304540d1.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186256376 :question/worked-solution "![](images/dc/dc75845932358d060d5878a30f00fa01e2b7b54408bca308110a53ea654ae18c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=1$ and $y=1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=1$ and $y=1$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>1$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x-1){y}^{2}-x+1=(x-1)(y+1)(y-1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=1$ and $y=\\pm 1.$\n\n- Equation II gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy-y-(x-1)=(x-1)(y-1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=1$ and $y=1,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y-1-x(y-1)=(1-x)(y-1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=1$ and $y=1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>1$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "![](images/dc/dc75845932358d060d5878a30f00fa01e2b7b54408bca308110a53ea654ae18c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=1$ and $y=1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=1$ and $y=1$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>1$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=(x-1){y}^{2}-x+1$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x-1){y}^{2}-x+1=(x-1)(y+1)(y-1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=1$ and $y=\\pm 1.$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=xy-y-(x-1)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy-y-(x-1)=(x-1)(y-1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=1$ and $y=1,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=y-1-x(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y-1-x(y-1)=(1-x)(y-1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=1$ and $y=1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>1$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=xy-y-(x-1)$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186462733 :answer-field/key "I" "${y}^{′}=(x-1){y}^{2}-x+1$"] [:db/cas 17592186462734 :answer-field/key "II" "${y}^{′}=xy-y-(x-1)$"] [:db/cas 17592186462735 :answer-field/key "III" "${y}^{′}=y-1-x(y-1)$"] [:db/cas 17592186256380 :question/problem "![](images/e9/e90d9a160db2c75091253eaa8a8c815e92c45f988841683f5a4d18ca6a6ea7db.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-x{y}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/e9/e90d9a160db2c75091253eaa8a8c815e92c45f988841683f5a4d18ca6a6ea7db.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186256380 :question/worked-solution "![](images/54/54932bcffbbe793d6e1f730e65570b19cfc3603ddb62eaf0a63ef2e5b0705979.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- For $y\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $x=0$ only.\n\n- For $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-x{y}^{2}$ evaluates to zero both on $x=0$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{x}$ does not evaluate to zero on $x=0.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. For $y\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y}$ evaluates to zero on $x=0$ only, and for $x\\ne 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{x}{y}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "![](images/54/54932bcffbbe793d6e1f730e65570b19cfc3603ddb62eaf0a63ef2e5b0705979.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- For $y\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $x=0$ only.\n\n- For $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=-x{y}^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-x{y}^{2}$ evaluates to zero both on $x=0$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{x}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{x}$ does not evaluate to zero on $x=0.$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y}$ gives the required behavior. For $y\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y}$ evaluates to zero on $x=0$ only, and for $x\\ne 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{x}{y}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186462736 :answer-field/key "I" "${y}^{′}=-x{y}^{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186462737 :answer-field/key "II" "${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{x}$"] [:db/cas 17592186462738 :answer-field/key "III" "${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y}$"] [:db/cas 17592186256382 :question/problem "![](images/e8/e8b573c6c2c59123de46346ccfc717fa89a98f7838e643a5e82972d753383588.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y+1}{x}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/e8/e8b573c6c2c59123de46346ccfc717fa89a98f7838e643a5e82972d753383588.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186256382 :question/worked-solution "![](images/2f/2f654bc96d27ac53dd501b10c5beb0d1c7d04ff4f643c5044692afac26ca2ba8.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- For $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=0$ only.\n\n- For $y\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I gives the required behavior. For $x\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0$ only, and for $y\\ne 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0.$\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. For $x\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y+1}{x}$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "![](images/2f/2f654bc96d27ac53dd501b10c5beb0d1c7d04ff4f643c5044692afac26ca2ba8.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- For $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=0$ only.\n\n- For $y\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ gives the required behavior. For $x\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0$ only, and for $y\\ne 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0.$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y+1}{x}$ does *not* give the required behavior. For $x\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y+1}{x}$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186462739 :answer-field/key "I" "${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$"] [:db/cas 17592186462740 :answer-field/key "II" "${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$"] [:db/cas 17592186462741 :answer-field/key "III" "${y}^{′}=\\frac{y+1}{x}$"] [:db/cas 17592186256390 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,1),(3,3),$ and $(5,-2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of a vertex of ${S}^{′}$, the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n- **I.** $(5,-2)$\n- **II.** $(-2,-5)$\n- **III.** $(3,12)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,1),(3,3),$ and $(5,-2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of a vertex of ${S}^{′}$, the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186256390 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-2,-5),(3,12),(5,13).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-2,-5),(3,12),(5,13).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is “The point $(-2,-5)$”; “The point $(3,12)$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186462742 :answer-field/key "I" "The point $(5,-2)$"] [:db/cas 17592186462743 :answer-field/key "II" "The point $(-2,-5)$"] [:db/cas 17592186462744 :answer-field/key "III" "The point $(3,12)$"] [:db/cas 17592186256410 :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n- **I.** $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}$\n- **II.** $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{6}{7}$\n- **III.** $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186256410 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, equations I, II, and III have no solutions." "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, equations $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}$, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{6}{7}$, and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{3}$ have no solutions."] [:db/cas 17592186462745 :answer-field/key "I" "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}$"] [:db/cas 17592186462746 :answer-field/key "II" "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{6}{7}$"] [:db/cas 17592186462747 :answer-field/key "III" "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186256411 :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}?$\n- **I.** $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{2}$\n- **II.** $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{5}{8}$\n- **III.** $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following equations has no solutions for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186256411 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equations II and III have no solutions." "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equations $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{5}{8}$ and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ have no solutions."] [:db/cas 17592186462748 :answer-field/key "I" "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186462749 :answer-field/key "II" "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{5}{8}$"] [:db/cas 17592186462750 :answer-field/key "III" "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186256412 :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n- **I.** $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}$$$\n- **II.** $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{5}{3}$\n- **III.** $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2}{5}$\n\