t IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative." "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2(e-1),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$” is true. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,e].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}⟨1,\\,t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}1+1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2t\\,{|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =2(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2(e-1),$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the segment line $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$” is false. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨t(e-1)+1,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨1,\\,t(e-1)+1⟩\\cdot ⟨e-1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}e-1+t(e-1)+1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t(e-1)+e\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}(e-1)}{2}+et]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-1}{2}+e \\\\  & =\\frac{3e-1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path” is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative."] [:db/cas 17592186464705 :answer-field/key "I" "${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2(e-1),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$"] [:db/cas 17592186464706 :answer-field/key "II" "${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2(e-1),$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the segment line $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$"] [:db/cas 17592186464707 :answer-field/key "III" "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path."] [:db/cas 17592186464708 :answer-field/key "IV" "$F$ is conservative."] [:db/cas 17592186259864 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2},\\,-x⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(4,2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{5}{6},$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{16}{3},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n- **IV.** $F$ is conservative." "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2},\\,-x⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(4,2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186259864 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨4t,\\,2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨4{t}^{2},\\,-4t⟩\\cdot ⟨4,\\,2⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}16{t}^{2}-8t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{t}^{2}-t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{[\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}⟨{t}^{2},\\,-{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3})-0 \\\\  & =8-\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative." "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{5}{6},$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is false. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨4t,\\,2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨4{t}^{2},\\,-4t⟩\\cdot ⟨4,\\,2⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}16{t}^{2}-8t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{t}^{2}-t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{[\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{16}{3},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is true. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}⟨{t}^{2},\\,-{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3})-0 \\\\  & =8-\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path” is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative."] [:db/cas 17592186449160 :answer-field/key "I" "${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{5}{6},$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186449161 :answer-field/key "II" "${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{16}{3},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186449162 :answer-field/key "III" "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path."] [:db/cas 17592186449163 :answer-field/key "IV" "$F$ is conservative."] [:db/cas 17592186262582 :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n- **I.** The volume, measured in cubic centimeters, of water in a cup of coffee\n- **II.** The average length, measured in millimeters, of hairs on a horse\n- **III.** The number of hairs on a cat\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262582 :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/05/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,…,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/05/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “The volume, measured in cubic centimeters, of water in a cup of coffee” describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement “The average length, measured in millimeters, of hairs on a horse” describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement “The number of hairs on a cat” does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,…,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The volume, measured in cubic centimeters, of water in a cup of coffee”; “The average length, measured in millimeters, of hairs on a horse” only."] [:db/cas 17592186464709 :answer-field/key "I" "The volume, measured in cubic centimeters, of water in a cup of coffee"] [:db/cas 17592186464710 :answer-field/key "II" "The average length, measured in millimeters, of hairs on a horse"] [:db/cas 17592186464711 :answer-field/key "III" "The number of hairs on a cat"] [:db/cas 17592186262583 :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n- **I.** The number obtained when throwing a die\n- **II.** The weight, measured in grams, of sugar in a bowl\n- **III.** The speed limit on a certain road\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262583 :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/05/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $1,2,3,4,5,6,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a continuous random variable. The speed limit on a certain road is not the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/05/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “The number obtained when throwing a die” does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $1,2,3,4,5,6,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement “The weight, measured in grams, of sugar in a bowl” describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement “The speed limit on a certain road” does *not* describe a continuous random variable. The speed limit on a certain road is not the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The weight, measured in grams, of sugar in a bowl” only."] [:db/cas 17592186464712 :answer-field/key "I" "The number obtained when throwing a die"] [:db/cas 17592186464713 :answer-field/key "II" "The weight, measured in grams, of sugar in a bowl"] [:db/cas 17592186464714 :answer-field/key "III" "The speed limit on a certain road"] [:db/cas 17592186262584 :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n- **I.** The percentage of impurity in a batch of chemicals\n- **II.** The area of an equilateral triangle with a side of $2$\n- **III.** The amount of snowfall, in centimeters, in a randomly selected city in January 2022\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262584 :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/05/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I  describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,100]$ (since the percentage can be anywhere between $0%$ and $100%$).\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a continuous random variable. The area of an equilateral triangle with a given side length is not the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/05/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “The percentage of impurity in a batch of chemicals”  describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,100]$ (since the percentage can be anywhere between $0%$ and $100%$).\n\n- Statement “The area of an equilateral triangle with a side of $2$” does *not* describe a continuous random variable. The area of an equilateral triangle with a given side length is not the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\n- Statement “The amount of snowfall, in centimeters, in a randomly selected city in January 2022” describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The percentage of impurity in a batch of chemicals”; “The amount of snowfall, in centimeters, in a randomly selected city in January 2022” only."] [:db/cas 17592186464715 :answer-field/key "I" "The percentage of impurity in a batch of chemicals"] [:db/cas 17592186464716 :answer-field/key "II" "The area of an equilateral triangle with a side of $2$"] [:db/cas 17592186464717 :answer-field/key "III" "The amount of snowfall, in centimeters, in a randomly selected city in January 2022"] [:db/cas 17592186262585 :question/problem "For the sets $X={x,y,z},$ $Y={v,w},$ and $Z=X\\times Y,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(x,v)\\in Z$\n- **II.** $(y,w)\\in Z$\n- **III.** $(w,z)\\in Z$" "For the sets $X={x,y,z},$ $Y={v,w},$ and $Z=X\\times Y,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262585 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $X\\times Y$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $X$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $Y.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $X\\times Y$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/31/316a12102e4be81291f06403815cfb63749b1fd3f463c465294e031543123484.png)\n\nHence, the set $Z=X\\times Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nZ={(x,v),(x,w),(y,v),(y,w),(z,v),(z,w)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "The Cartesian product $X\\times Y$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $X$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $Y.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $X\\times Y$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/31/316a12102e4be81291f06403815cfb63749b1fd3f463c465294e031543123484.png)\n\nHence, the set $Z=X\\times Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nZ={(x,v),(x,w),(y,v),(y,w),(z,v),(z,w)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(x,v)\\in Z$; $(y,w)\\in Z$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186449164 :answer-field/key "I" "$(x,v)\\in Z$"] [:db/cas 17592186449165 :answer-field/key "II" "$(y,w)\\in Z$"] [:db/cas 17592186449166 :answer-field/key "III" "$(w,z)\\in Z$"] [:db/cas 17592186262598 :question/problem "Which of the following are zero matrices?\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$H=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$G=\\begin{bmatrix}0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following are zero matrices?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186464718 :answer-field/key "G" "$G=\\begin{bmatrix}0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464719 :answer-field/key "H" "$H=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464720 :answer-field/key "I" "$I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186262599 :question/problem "Which of the following are zero matrices?\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$R=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$S=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$T=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following are zero matrices?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186464721 :answer-field/key "R" "$R=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464722 :answer-field/key "S" "$S=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464723 :answer-field/key "T" "$T=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186262600 :question/problem "Which of the following are square matrices?\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$K=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$L=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$M=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Which of the following are square matrices?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186464724 :answer-field/key "K" "$K=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464725 :answer-field/key "L" "$L=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464726 :answer-field/key "M" "$M=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186262685 :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\nI. $f(x)=2|x|$\n\nII. $g(x)=|x-1|$\n\nIII. $h(x)=|x+1|$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262685 :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=2|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1],$ but it has a corner in $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x-1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=1-x$ over the given interval. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $h(x)=|x+1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=x+1$ over the given interval. Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=h(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only functions II and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=2|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1],$ but it has a corner in $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x-1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=1-x$ over the given interval. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $h(x)=|x+1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=x+1$ over the given interval. Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=h(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only functions $g(x)=|x-1|$ and $h(x)=|x+1|$ satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$"] [:db/cas 17592186464727 :answer-field/key "I" "$f(x)=2|x|$"] [:db/cas 17592186464728 :answer-field/key "II" "$g(x)=|x-1|$"] [:db/cas 17592186464729 :answer-field/key "III" "$h(x)=|x+1|$"] [:db/cas 17592186262686 :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3]?$\n- **I.** $p(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- **II.** $q(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$\n- **III.** $r(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x-1|)$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3]?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186262686 :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3],$ the function must be continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $p(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$ Therefore, $p(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(1,\\infty ).$ Given that it is not defined at $x=1,$ $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$ Given that it is not defined at $x=1,$ $r(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function I satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3].$" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3],$ the function must be continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $p(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$ Therefore, $p(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(1,\\infty ).$ Given that it is not defined at $x=1,$ $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$ Given that it is not defined at $x=1,$ $r(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function $p(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3].$"] [:db/cas 17592186464730 :answer-field/key "I" "$p(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$"] [:db/cas 17592186464731 :answer-field/key "II" "$q(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$"] [:db/cas 17592186464732 :answer-field/key "III" "$r(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x-1|)$"] [:db/cas 17592186262687 :question/problem "![](images/95/95e7e2df02e4694d85be868f348bb65fb6a7bf2c646b89000b9af655f937a12f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/95/95e7e2df02e4694d85be868f348bb65fb6a7bf2c646b89000b9af655f937a12f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262687 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement III is false. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/bf/bfd6a4f3d368bc5920e1c79eed913f4ef21fcdf8b9eb42bbc5ce3f5c2ba7d36b.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/78/789b387e512c53895b0cfa499c70547046318a29da6d9af7f556a07bcac830ed.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive” is true, while statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” is false. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/bf/bfd6a4f3d368bc5920e1c79eed913f4ef21fcdf8b9eb42bbc5ce3f5c2ba7d36b.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative” is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/78/789b387e512c53895b0cfa499c70547046318a29da6d9af7f556a07bcac830ed.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive” only."] [:db/cas 17592186464733 :answer-field/key "I" "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive"] [:db/cas 17592186464734 :answer-field/key "II" "The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative"] [:db/cas 17592186464735 :answer-field/key "III" "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero"] [:db/cas 17592186262688 :question/problem "![](images/06/06c6dd24948a95d08850455d273c3456076850a47c980a086f06813b96bfd6ed.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/06/06c6dd24948a95d08850455d273c3456076850a47c980a086f06813b96bfd6ed.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262688 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement III is true. From the diagram below we see that $F$ acts perpendicular to $n$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/29/29f144d6f3ce8233a034025fe933bc57dbea5476c50a259853a98bd2a2b3a47b.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/d7/d7a469a331a3cda238fcefb9e7163743483975c4ecc7b962d6d5f8d79736693c.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative” is false, while statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” is true. From the diagram below we see that $F$ acts perpendicular to $n$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/29/29f144d6f3ce8233a034025fe933bc57dbea5476c50a259853a98bd2a2b3a47b.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive” is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/d7/d7a469a331a3cda238fcefb9e7163743483975c4ecc7b962d6d5f8d79736693c.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” only."] [:db/cas 17592186464736 :answer-field/key "I" "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative"] [:db/cas 17592186464737 :answer-field/key "II" "The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive"] [:db/cas 17592186464738 :answer-field/key "III" "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero"] [:db/cas 17592186262690 :question/problem "![](images/65/6504ab3dde96670102e5046887707416495895dc3b85a514b5bbe7b526b4fa42.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/65/6504ab3dde96670102e5046887707416495895dc3b85a514b5bbe7b526b4fa42.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262690 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement III is false. Note that the flux $F$ that flows in through $C$ is the same that flows out through $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/9a/9af12f28947e46810074574dad9f6b9a8b572f116456807bf9c478d12da19ec2.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/c4/c4402595afc4ce12937794f0755a446ae738d3b47d7e9f4978b6344f9ef8909a.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escap