intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement “The coefficient matrix is not invertible” is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “The determinant of the coefficient matrix is not zero” is true. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement “The lines $x+2y=-11$ and $y+7x=4$ intersect at infinitely many  points” is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement “The determinant of the coefficient matrix is not zero” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186449170 :answer-field/key "I" "The coefficient matrix is not invertible"] [:db/cas 17592186449171 :answer-field/key "II" "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is not zero"] [:db/cas 17592186449172 :answer-field/key "III" "The lines $x+2y=-11$ and $y+7x=4$ intersect at infinitely many  points"] [:db/cas 17592186262851 :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=b$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n\n- **II.** $A$ is  invertible\n\n- **III.** $Ax=0$ has a nonzero solution" "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=b$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262851 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement $det(A)\\ne 0$ is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is  invertible” is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “$Ax=0$ has a nonzero solution” is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements $det(A)\\ne 0$ and “$A$ is  invertible” are true."] [:db/cas 17592186449173 :answer-field/key "I" "$det(A)\\ne 0$"] [:db/cas 17592186449174 :answer-field/key "II" "$A$ is  invertible"] [:db/cas 17592186449175 :answer-field/key "III" "$Ax=0$ has a nonzero solution"] [:db/cas 17592186262852 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y &=-1 \\\\ 3y-x &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The lines $2x-2y=-1$ and $3y-x=1$ intersect at infinitely many  points\n- **II.** The coefficient matrix is not  invertible\n- **III.** The determinant of the coefficient matrix is not equal to zero" "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y &=-1 \\\\ 3y-x &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262852 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement “The lines $2x-2y=-1$ and $3y-x=1$ intersect at infinitely many  points” is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “The coefficient matrix is not  invertible” is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “The determinant of the coefficient matrix is not equal to zero” is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement “The determinant of the coefficient matrix is not equal to zero” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186449176 :answer-field/key "I" "The lines $2x-2y=-1$ and $3y-x=1$ intersect at infinitely many  points"] [:db/cas 17592186449177 :answer-field/key "II" "The coefficient matrix is not  invertible"] [:db/cas 17592186449178 :answer-field/key "III" "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is not equal to zero"] [:db/cas 17592186262854 :question/problem "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -3.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- **II.** $Ax=0$ has no solutions\n- **III.** The lines $x+3y=-1$ and $-x+ky=1$  intersect at exactly one point." "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -3.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262854 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =k+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $k+3\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -3.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the unique solution to  $Ax=0$ is the trivial one (zero).\n\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statements I and III are true." "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement $det(A)\\ne 0$ is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =k+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $k+3\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -3.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement “$Ax=0$ has no solutions” is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the unique solution to  $Ax=0$ is the trivial one (zero).\n\n- Statement “The lines $x+3y=-1$ and $-x+ky=1$  intersect at exactly one point” is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statements $det(A)\\ne 0$ and “The lines $x+3y=-1$ and $-x+ky=1$  intersect at exactly one point” are true."] [:db/cas 17592186449179 :answer-field/key "I" "$det(A)\\ne 0$"] [:db/cas 17592186449180 :answer-field/key "II" "$Ax=0$ has no solutions"] [:db/cas 17592186449181 :answer-field/key "III" "The lines $x+3y=-1$ and $-x+ky=1$  intersect at exactly one point."] [:db/cas 17592186262856 :question/problem "Let \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- **II.** $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$\n- **III.** ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist" "Let \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262856 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the first part of the invertible matrix theorem, the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ must exist.\n\nFinally, only statements I and II are true." "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement $det(A)\\ne 0$ is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement “$Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$” is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist” is false. According to the first part of the invertible matrix theorem, the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ must exist.\n\nFinally, only statements $det(A)\\ne 0$ and “$Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$” are true."] [:db/cas 17592186449182 :answer-field/key "I" "$det(A)\\ne 0$"] [:db/cas 17592186449183 :answer-field/key "II" "$Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$"] [:db/cas 17592186449184 :answer-field/key "III" "${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist"] [:db/cas 17592186262876 :question/problem "Let \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)=0$\n- **II.** $A$ is invertible\n- **III.** $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$" "Let \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262876 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that 