lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement II is true." "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement $det(A)=0$ is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 14 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =7\\cdot k-(-2)\\cdot 14 \\\\  & =7k+28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $7k+28\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -4.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement “The system  $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution” is true. Indeed, by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is the trivial one (zero).\n\n- Statement “The lines $7x+14y=2$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at infinitely many points” is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement “The system  $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186449188 :answer-field/key "I" "$det(A)=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186449189 :answer-field/key "II" "The system  $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution"] [:db/cas 17592186449190 :answer-field/key "III" "The lines $7x+14y=2$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at infinitely many points"] [:db/cas 17592186262879 :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the system $\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}x=0$ has the unique zero solution, then $\