hat in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option $det\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0$ gives us a true statement:  \n\n*If the system $\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}x=0$ has the unique zero solution, then, $det\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0$ .* $\\,✓$"] [:db/cas 17592186464766 :answer-field/key "I" "${\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186464767 :answer-field/key "II" "The lines $ax+by=0$ and $cx+dy=0$ intersect at infinitely many points"] [:db/cas 17592186464768 :answer-field/key "III" "$det\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0$"] [:db/cas 17592186262880 :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times 2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$ . *\n- **I.** $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution\n- **II.** The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n- **III.** $det(A)=0$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times 2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$ . *\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262880 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and II give us true statements: \n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution.* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.* $\\,✓$" "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options “$Ax=0$ has exactly one solution” and “The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point” give us true statements: \n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution.* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.* $\\,✓$"] [:db/cas 17592186464769 :answer-field/key "I" "$Ax=0$ has exactly one solution"] [:db/cas 17592186464770 :answer-field/key "II" "The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point."] [:db/cas 17592186464771 :answer-field/key "III" "$det(A)=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186262906 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $Q$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(Q)=-5$\n- **II.** $det(Q)=1$\n- **III.** $Q$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $Q$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186438923 :answer-field/key "I" "$det(Q)=-5$"] [:db/cas 17592186438924 :answer-field/key "II" "$det(Q)=1$"] [:db/cas 17592186438925 :answer-field/key "III" "$Q$ is **not** a singular matrix"] [:db/cas 17592186262908 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $T$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $T$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $T$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(T)=-30$\n\n \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $T$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262908 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-5|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(1-3)-3(2-1)-5(6-1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-2-3-25 \\\\  & =-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(T)=-30\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true." "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-5|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(1-3)-3(2-1)-5(6-1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-2-3-25 \\\\  & =-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(T)=-30\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$T$ is **not** a singular matrix” and $det(T)=-30$ are true."] [:db/cas 17592186438926 :answer-field/key "I" "$T$ is a singular matrix"] [:db/cas 17592186438927 :answer-field/key "II" "$T$ is **not** a singular matrix"] [:db/cas 17592186438928 :answer-field/key "III" "$det(T)=-30$"] [:db/cas 17592186262913 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $R$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $R$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $det(R)=0$\n- **III.** $det(R)=1$\n\n \n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $R$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262913 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{aligned}|-\\sqrt{2}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(0+\\sqrt{2})-\\sqrt{2}(2+1)+2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3+2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(R)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{aligned}|-\\sqrt{2}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(0+\\sqrt{2})-\\sqrt{2}(2+1)+2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3+2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(R)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$R$ is a singular matrix” and $det(R)=0$ are true."] [:db/cas 17592186438935 :answer-field/key "I" "$R$ is a singular matrix"] [:db/cas 17592186438936 :answer-field/key "II" "$det(R)=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186438937 :answer-field/key "III" "$det(R)=1$"] [:db/cas 17592186262919 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ find the points $(x,y),$ for $0\\le y<\\pi ,$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $(-1,0)$\n- **II.** $(0,\\frac{\\pi}{2})$\n- **III.** $(1,0)$\n- **IV.** $(1,\\frac{\\pi}{2})$" "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ find the points $(x,y),$ for $0\\le y<\\pi ,$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186440105 :answer-field/key "I" "$(-1,0)$"] [:db/cas 17592186440106 :answer-field/key "II" "$(0,\\frac{\\pi}{2})$"] [:db/cas 17592186440107 :answer-field/key "III" "$(1,0)$"] [:db/cas 17592186440108 :answer-field/key "IV" "$(1,\\frac{\\pi}{2})$"] [:db/cas 17592186262933 :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+{y}^{2}}{x}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $c=-1$ gives a line\n\n- **II.** $c=0$ gives a parabola, excluding the origin\n\n- **III.** $c=1$ gives the $x$-axis, excluding the origin" "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+{y}^{2}}{x}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262933 :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+{y}^{2}}{x} & =c \\\\ x+{y}^{2} & =cx \\\\ x(1-c)+{y}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=-1,$ the level curve is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2},$ which is a parabola that opens to the left (excluding the origin). So, I is false.\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curves is $x=-{y}^{2},$ which is a parabola that opens to the left (excluding the origin). So, II is true.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curve is $y=0,$ which is the $x$-axis (excluding the origin). So, III is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+{y}^{2}}{x} & =c \\\\ x+{y}^{2} & =cx \\\\ x(1-c)+{y}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=-1,$ the level curve is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2},$ which is a parabola that opens to the left (excluding the origin). So, “$c=-1$ gives a line” is false.\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curves is $x=-{y}^{2},$ which is a parabola that opens to the left (excluding the origin). So, “$c=0$ gives a parabola, excluding the origin” is true.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curve is $y=0,$ which is the $x$-axis (excluding the origin). So, “$c=1$ gives the $x$-axis, excluding the origin” is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is “$c=0$ gives a parabola, excluding the origin”; “$c=1$ gives the $x$-axis, excluding the origin” only."] [:db/cas 17592186449191 :answer-field/key "I" "$c=-1$ gives a line"] [:db/cas 17592186449192 :answer-field/key "II" "$c=0$ gives a parabola, excluding the origin"] [:db/cas 17592186449193 :answer-field/key "III" "$c=1$ gives the $x$-axis, excluding the origin"] [:db/cas 17592186262934 :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $c=0$ gives a parabola\n- **II.** $c=1$ gives an ellipse\n- **III.** $c=2$ gives a hyperbola\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186262934 :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ {y}^{2} & =c({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\ c{x}^{2}+(c-1){y}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curve is the line $y=0,$ which is the $x$-axis. So, I is false.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curve is the line $x=0,$ which is the $y$-axis. So, II is false.\n\n- If $c=2,$  then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=2\\,⟹\\,2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, the only real solution to this equation is  $(x,y)=(0,0),$ which is not in the domain of $f(x,y)$ because it would result in division by zero. Thus, III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the listed\"." "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ {y}^{2} & =c({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\ c{x}^{2}+(c-1){y}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curve is the line $y=0,$ which is the $x$-axis. So, “$c=0$ gives a parabola” is false.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curve is the line $x=0,$ which is the $y$-axis. So, “$c=1$ gives an ellipse” is false.\n\n- If $c=2,$  then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=2\\,⟹\\,2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, the only real solution to this equation is  $(x,y)=(0,0),$ which is not in the domain of $f(x,y)$ because it would result in division by zero. Thus, “$c=2$ gives a hyperbola” is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the listed\"."] [:db/cas 17592186464772 :answer-field/key "I" "$c=0$ gives a parabola"] [:db/cas 17592186464773 :answer-field/key "II" "$c=1$ gives an ellipse"] [:db/cas 17592186464774 :answer-field/key "III" "$c=2$ gives a hyperbola"] [:db/cas 17592186262935 :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $c=-1$ gives a hyperbola\n- **II.** $c=0$ gives the $y$-axis, excluding the origin\n- **III.** $c=1$ gives the line $y=x,$ excluding the origin" "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262935 :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & =c \\\\ y & =cx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=-1,$ the level curve is the line $y=-x,$ excluding the origin. So, I is false.\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curve is the line $y=0,$ which is the $x$-axis (excluding the origin). So, II is false.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curve is the line $y=x,$ excluding the origin. So, III is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & =c \\\\ y & =cx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=-1,$ the level curve is the line $y=-x,$ excluding the origi