in{array}{ccc|c}0 & 9 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186464805 :answer-field/key "A" "$A=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}4 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]$"] [:db/cas 17592186464806 :answer-field/key "B" "$B=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 7 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]$"] [:db/cas 17592186464807 :answer-field/key "C" "$C=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 9 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{array}\\right]$"] [:db/cas 17592186262996 :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & -4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 7\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-6 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n$$\nC=\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}0 & 1 & -4 & 5 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following augmented matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186464808 :answer-field/key "A" "$A=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & -4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 7\\end{array}\\right]$"] [:db/cas 17592186464809 :answer-field/key "B" "$B=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-6 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{array}\\right]$"] [:db/cas 17592186464810 :answer-field/key "C" "$C=\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}0 & 1 & -4 & 5 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5 & 1\\end{array}\\right]$"] [:db/cas 17592186262997 :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 6\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}9 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n$$\nC=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following augmented matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186464811 :answer-field/key "A" "$A=\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 6\\end{array}\\right]$"] [:db/cas 17592186464812 :answer-field/key "B" "$B=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}9 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]$"] [:db/cas 17592186464813 :answer-field/key "C" "$C=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 4\\end{array}\\right]$"] [:db/cas 17592186262999 :question/problem "Given the two planes $2x-5y+z+1=0$ and $3x-z+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- **II.** The planes are parallel.\n- **III.** The planes intersect on a line." "Given the two planes $2x-5y+z+1=0$ and $3x-z+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262999 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-5y+z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-5,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x-z+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-5,1⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =5i+5j+15k \\\\  & =⟨5,5,15⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-5y+z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-5,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x-z+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement “The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$” is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-5,1⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =5i+5j+15k \\\\  & =⟨5,5,15⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The planes are parallel” is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186449197 :answer-field/key "I" "The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186449198 :answer-field/key "II" "The planes are parallel."] [:db/cas 17592186449199 :answer-field/key "III" "The planes intersect on a line."] [:db/cas 17592186263000 :question/problem "Given the two planes $3x-y+2z=0$ and $9x-3y+6z+5=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- **II.** The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n- **III.** The planes are coincident." "Given the two planes $3x-y+2z=0$ and $9x-3y+6z+5=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186263000 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x-y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-1,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $9x-3y+6z+5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨9,-3,6⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${n}_{2}=3{n}_{1}.$ So, the normal vectors are parallel.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. \n\nFor the two planes to coincide, the equation of one plane should be a scalar multiple of the other. However, if we try to manipulate the equation of the second plane to resemble the equation of the first plane, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x-3y+6z+5=0 \\\\ 3x-y+2z+\\frac{5}{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nClearly, this equation is not the same as the equation of the first plane. So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x-y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-1,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $9x-3y+6z+5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨9,-3,6⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement “The normal vectors of the planes are parallel” is true. Indeed, ${n}_{2}=3{n}_{1}.$ So, the normal vectors are parallel.\n\n- Statement “The planes are parallel but not coincident” is true, while statement “The planes are coincident” is false. \n\nFor the two planes to coincide, the equation of one plane should be a scalar multiple of the other. However, if we try to manipulate the equation of the second plane to resemble the equation of the first plane, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x-3y+6z+5=0 \\\\ 3x-y+2z+\\frac{5}{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nClearly, this equation is not the same as the equation of the first plane. So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statements “The normal vectors of the planes are parallel” and “The planes are parallel but not coincident” are true."] [:db/cas 17592186449200 :answer-field/key "I" "The normal vectors of the planes are parallel."] [:db/cas 17592186449201 :answer-field/key "II" "The planes are parallel but not coincident."] [:db/cas 17592186449202 :answer-field/key "III" "The planes are coincident."] [:db/cas 17592186263001 :question/problem "Given the two planes $x-3y+2z=0$ and $x-2y+5=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n\n- **II.** The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n\n- **III.** The planes intersect on a line." "Given the two planes $x-3y+2z=0$ and $x-2y+5=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186263001 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-3y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-3,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-2,1⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨4,2,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-3y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-3,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements “The normal vectors of the planes are parallel” and “The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$” are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-2,1⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨4,2,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186449203 :answer-field/key "I" "The normal vectors of the planes are parallel."] [:db/cas 17592186449204 :answer-field/key "II" "The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186449205 :answer-field/key "III" "The planes intersect on a line."] [:db/cas 17592186263002 :question/problem "Given the two planes $x-5y+2z=0$ and $3x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n\n- **II.** The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n\n- **III.** The planes intersect on a line." "Given the two planes $x-5y+2z=0$ and $3x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186263002 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-5y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-5,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-5,2⟩\\times ⟨3,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+6j+13k \\\\  & =⟨4,6,13⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-5y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-5,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements “The normal vectors of the planes are parallel” and “The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$” are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-5,2⟩\\times ⟨3,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+6j+13k \\\\  & =⟨4,6,13⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186449206 :answer-field/key "I" "The normal vectors of the planes are parallel."] [:db/cas 17592186449207 :answer-field/key "II" "The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186449208 :answer-field/key "III" "The planes intersect on a line."] [:db/cas 17592186263003 :question/problem "Given the two planes $2x-y+3z=0$ and $5x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨7,15,2⟩.$\n- **II.** The planes intersect on a line.\n- **III.** The planes are coincident." "Given the two planes $2x-y+3z=0$ and $5x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186263003 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\times ⟨5,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+15j+k \\\\  & =⟨6,15,1⟩\\ne ⟨7,15,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes cannot be coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement “The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨7,15,2⟩.$” is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\times ⟨5,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+15j+k \\\\  & =⟨6,15,1⟩\\ne ⟨7,15,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement “The planes are coincident” is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes cannot be coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186449209 :answer-field/key "I" "The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨7,15,2⟩.$"] [:db/cas 17592186449210 :answer-field/key "II" "The planes intersect on a line."] [:db/cas 17592186449211 :answer-field/key "III" "The planes are coincident."] [:db/cas 17592186265703 :question/problem "Given the two planes $3x+y-2z=0$ and $2x+y+3z+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨5,-13,1⟩.$\n- **II.** The planes are parallel.\n- **III.** The planes intersect on a line." "Given the two planes $3x+y-2z=0$ and $2x+y+3z+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186265703 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x+y-2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,1,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+y+3z+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,1,3⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,1,-2⟩\\times ⟨2,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =5i-13j+k \\\\  & =⟨5,-13,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the corresponding planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only I and III only are true." "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x+y-2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,1,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+y+3z+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,1,3⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement “The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨5,-13,1⟩.$” is true. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,1,-2⟩\\times ⟨2,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =5i-13j+k \\\\  & =⟨5,-13,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The planes are parallel” is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the corresponding planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only “The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨5,-13,1⟩.$” and “The planes intersect on a line” only are true."] [:db/cas 17592186449212 :answer-field/key "I" "The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨5,-13,1⟩.$"] [:db/cas 17592186449213 :answer-field/key "II" "The planes are parallel."] [:db/cas 17592186449214 :answer-field/key "III" "The planes intersect on a line."] [:db/cas 17592186265704 :question/problem "Given the two planes $x-y+2z=0$ and $5x+y-13=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨-3,12,6⟩.$\n- **II.** The planes intersect on a line.\n- **III.** The planes are coincident." "Given the two planes $x-y+2z=0$ and $5x+y-13=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186265704 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-1,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x+y-13=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-1,2⟩\\times ⟨5,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2i+10j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,10,6⟩\\ne ⟨-3,12,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes could not be coincident either.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-1,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x+y-13=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement “The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨-3,12,6⟩.$” is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-1,2⟩\\times ⟨5,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2i+10j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,10,6⟩\\ne ⟨-3,12,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement “The planes are coincident” is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes could not be coincident either.\n\nTherefore, only statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186449215 :answer-field/key "I" "The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨-3,12,6⟩.$"] [:db/cas 17592186449216 :answer-field/key "II" "The planes intersect on a line."] [:db/cas 17592186449217 :answer-field/key "III" "The planes are coincident."] [:db/cas 17592186265721 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y=0$\n- **II.** The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y=x$\n- **III.** The limit does not exist" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186265721 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2({0}^{2})-{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+2({0}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2({0}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is also true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{3\\cancel{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2({0}^{2})-{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+2({0}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y=0$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2({0}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y=x$” is also true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{3\\cancel{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit does not exist” is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186464814 :answer-field/key "I" "The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186464815 :answer-field/key "II" "The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y=x$"] [:db/cas 17592186464816 :answer-field/key "III" "The limit does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186265722 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=0$\n- **II.** The limit evaluates to $\\frac{2}{3}$ along the path $y=x$\n- **III.** The limit does not exist" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186265722 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2(0)(0)}{3({0}^{2})-{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x(0)}{3({0}^{2})-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x(x)}{3{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2(0)(0)}{3({0}^{2})-{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=0$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x(0)}{3({0}^{2})-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $\\frac{2}{3}$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x(x)}{3{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit does not exist” is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186449218 :answer-field/key "I" "The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186449219 :answer-field/key "II" "The limit evaluates to $\\frac{2}{3}$ along the path $y=x$"] [:db/cas 17592186449220 :answer-field/key "III" "The limit does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186265723 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The limit evaluates to $4$ along the path $y={x}^{4}$\n- **II.** The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $x=0$\n- **III.** The limit does not exist" "Which of the following statements are true regar